Package: plugin-hostd
Architecture: any
Depends: libplugin-hostd-protocol0 (>= 0.5.8~), libplugin-hostd-protocol0 (<< 0.6~), plugin-hostd-contract (= ${source:Version}), ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: omx-clap-host (>= 0.32.3~), omx-clap-host (<< 0.33~)
# mod-host runs an LV2 plugin only when lv2_worker or lv2_worker_set names it, and it is the reference host H3 of the
# benchmark (bench/run.sh), measured when it is installed. Nothing else uses it, so it is only suggested; a machine that
# runs a published benchmark installs mod-host (and jalv) itself.
Suggests: mod-host
Description: run LV2 and CLAP plugins so that one crash cannot stop the show
 plugin-hostd keeps your effects and instruments running when one of them
 misbehaves. It gives a controller one mod-host socket and starts each plugin,
 or each named group of plugins, in a worker process of its own: omx-clap-host
 for CLAP and, through its LV2 adapter, for LV2. If a plugin crashes, only its
 worker goes down and the daemon brings it back. Audio never passes through the
 daemon, so it adds no latency: every worker is a JACK client.
 .
 A format whose worker is not installed refuses its adds; the other formats
 are unaffected. mod-host runs LV2 plugins only when you ask for it.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

# The whole plugin host stack in one install, on any Debian, Zynthian or not (docs/install.md): the daemon at its own
# version, its CLAP/LV2 worker, the OpenMixer plugins, the qualifier and the qualification records
# (plugin-qualification-records, one package built per architecture: the desk sets on amd64, the Raspberry Pi 5 set on
# arm64). Each a floor at the release this one is tested with and a ceiling below the next minor; the records' dated
# version (YYYYMMDDXX) below the next year's first (tests/full_ranges.py). The records are recommended until the
# package is in the channel; a hard dependency on it now would leave plugin-hostd-full uninstallable. The panel is
# recommended: `apt install --no-install-recommends plugin-hostd-full` is the headless install, with no panel.
Package: plugin-hostd-full
Section: metapackages
Architecture: all
Depends: plugin-hostd (= ${source:Version}),
         omx-clap-host (>= 0.32.3~), omx-clap-host (<< 0.33~),
         omx-plugins (>= 0.19.3~), omx-plugins (<< 0.20~),
         plugin-qualify (>= 0.20.1~), plugin-qualify (<< 0.21~),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: omx-panel (>= 0.13.0~), omx-panel (<< 0.14~),
            plugin-qualification-records (>= 2026110902~), plugin-qualification-records (<< 2027000000~)
Description: whole plugin host stack: daemon, workers, plugins, qualifier
 Installs plugin-hostd with everything it runs plugins with: omx-clap-host,
 the CLAP worker that also runs LV2 plugins through its adapter, the OpenMixer
 plugins, plugin-qualify, which measures each installed plugin before it is
 trusted live, and the qualification records of this machine's class, so the
 plugins measured on a machine like this one are taken from the records.
 .
 omx-panel, the plugin panels in a browser, is recommended. Install with
 --no-install-recommends for a machine with no screen.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: plugin-hostd-tests
Architecture: any
Depends: plugin-hostd (= ${binary:Version}), ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: stand-in plugin worker to test a program that drives plugin-hostd
 A small worker that speaks the same protocol as mod-host and omx-clap-host
 and can be made to die on cue, so a program that talks to plugin-hostd can
 be tested against a real daemon without a plugin, a JACK server or any
 audio. Run it with -L to print the layout it reports. It is installed in
 /usr/libexec/plugin-hostd/tests, away from the directories programs run
 from.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: plugin-hostd-dev
Section: libdevel
Architecture: all
Depends: plugin-hostd-contract (= ${source:Version}), libplugin-hostd-protocol-dev (>= 0.5.8~), libplugin-hostd-protocol-dev (<< 0.6~), plugin-db-dev (>= 0.4.1~), plugin-db-dev (<< 0.5~), ${misc:Depends}
Description: C headers to drive plugin-hostd from your own controller
 The words of the grouping planner and the running history,
 and the schemas of the probe output and of a preset file. The protocol a
 controller speaks (the verbs, error codes, feedback events and settings) is
 declared in libplugin-hostd-protocol-dev, which this package depends on:
 pkg-config plugin-hostd gives the flags for both.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: plugin-hostd-contract
Architecture: all
Depends: omx-clap-core-contract (>= 0.32.3~), omx-clap-core-contract (<< 0.33~), omx-contract (>= 3.12.0~), omx-contract (<< 4~), plugin-hostd-protocol-contract (>= 0.5.8~), plugin-hostd-protocol-contract (<< 0.6~),
         ${misc:Depends}
Breaks: plugin-hostd-dev (<< ${source:Version})
Replaces: plugin-hostd-dev (<< ${source:Version})
Description: plugin-hostd's format tables, read at run time
 plugin-hostd's package of omx-contract's contract directory: the format
 tables a scan maps a CLAP or LV2 plugin through (formats/clap.json,
 formats/lv2.json), the keys of the running history (history.json) and the
 reasons a plan gives for a plugin it leaves alone (grouping.json), under
 /usr/share/omx-contract/contract.d/3/plugin-hostd. A program that reads
 them at run time depends on this package, never on the development one.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

Package: python3-plugin-hostd-protocol
Architecture: all
Depends: python3, libplugin-hostd-protocol0 (>= 0.5.8~), libplugin-hostd-protocol0 (<< 0.6~),
         plugin-hostd-protocol-contract (>= 0.5.8~), plugin-hostd-protocol-contract (<< 0.6~),
         omx-clap-core-contract (>= 0.32.3~), omx-clap-core-contract (<< 0.33~),
         omx-contract (>= 3.12.0~), omx-contract (<< 4~),
         omx-contract-data (>= 3.16.0~), omx-contract-data (<< 4~), ${misc:Depends}
Description: Python client of plugin-hostd's protocol
 plugin_hostd_protocol: one codec over the files plugin-hostd-protocol
 installs, which encodes and decodes every command the protocol declares,
 parses the replies, feedback lines and protocol errors, and gives each reply
 code its sentence. A controller speaks the protocol through it and restates
 nothing; the npm package @openmixer/plugin-hostd-client is the same codec in
 TypeScript.
